\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a finite graph $H$, let $t_H(G)$ be the number of graph homomorphisms $H\to G$ divided by $|V(G)|^{|V(H)|}$. Characterize those $H$ for which, for each fixed density $p$, the two conditions $t_{K_2}(G_n)\to p$ and $t_H(G_n)\to p^{|E(H)|}$ force every finite graph homomorphism density to tend to its value in a random graph of density $p$.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2011/01}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For a finite graph $H$, let $t_H(G)$ be the number of graph homomorphisms $H\to G$ divided by $|V(G)|^{|V(H)|}$. Characterize those $H$ for which, for each fixed density $p$, the two conditions $t_{K_2}(G_n)\to p$ and $t_H(G_n)\to p^{|E(H)|}$ force every finite graph homomorphism density to tend to its value in a random graph of density $p$.

